**Current Requirements:**
```
SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) platform supporting multi-user operations with role hierarchy, temporary delegation, clearance-based authorization, and concurrent request processing. The system enforces security constraints such as clearance-based permissions, role delegation and revocation, and preserves safety, liveness, and integrity under concurrent operations. A new Emergency Module is to be integrated, enabling special operational modes and privileges during emergencies, with specific triggering, duration, and exit requirements. Emergency mode can be triggered exactly once per session by exactly 2 administrators, is active for an abstract fixed duration but may be ended early by the same 2 administrators, and grants special privileges only to the triggering administrators. While Emergency mode is active, new triggers are blocked. Roles delegated during Emergency mode are revoked upon exit, while roles delegated prior persist as per their policy.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
=======================
R1: Emergency Triggering  
Exactly 2 administrators, while the system is in Normal mode, are required to trigger an emergency, causing the system to enter Emergency mode. Each session may only trigger Emergency mode once, and no new emergency triggers are accepted while Emergency mode is active.

R2: Emergency Mode Duration  
Emergency mode remains active for an abstract fixed duration, unless ended earlier by the same 2 administrators who triggered it. The system must eventually return to Normal mode, either automatically after the fixed duration or manually if ended early.

R3: Modified Processing and Privileges During Emergency  
R3.1: During Emergency mode, request processing may bypass FIFO order to prioritize multiple high classification requests simultaneously.  
R3.2: During Emergency mode, users may temporarily hold mutually exclusive roles (as defined in the set of mutually exclusive abstract roles).  
R3.3: During Emergency mode, only the administrators who triggered Emergency mode temporarily hold emergency privileges, defined as an abstract set of permissions available exclusively during Emergency mode.

R4: Post-Emergency Restoration  
R4.1: After Emergency mode ends, emergency privileges must be revoked from the administrators who held them.  
R4.2: All roles that were delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked upon exit from Emergency mode. Roles delegated prior to Emergency mode (during Normal mode) remain active until their expiry or until revoked by an administrator.  
R4.3: Administrators who held emergency privileges must update their credentials after Emergency mode ends.

KEY CONCEPTS AND ENTITIES
==========================
- User: An actor in the system, associated with a clearance level.
- Administrator: A user with High clearance and special administrative privileges. Only administrators can trigger or end Emergency mode and receive emergency privileges if they are the triggering administrators.
- Role: A set of permissions that can be assigned or delegated to users.
- Delegated Role: A role temporarily granted to a user, subject to clearance and expiration/revocation. Delegated roles granted during Emergency mode are revoked upon exit from Emergency mode.
- Emergency Mode: A special system state with altered processing and privilege rules, entered upon a valid emergency trigger by exactly 2 administrators, lasting a fixed duration unless ended early by the same administrators. Only one Emergency mode may be active per session.
- Normal Mode: The default operational state of the system.
- Emergency Privileges: An abstract set of permissions granted only to the administrators who triggered Emergency mode, effective only during Emergency mode.
- Mutually Exclusive Roles: A predefined abstract set of roles that cannot normally be held by the same user, but may be simultaneously held during Emergency mode.
- Request: A user-submitted action affecting system objects, classified by the objects’ security classifications.
- Request Processing Order: The order in which submitted requests are processed (normally FIFO; can be altered in Emergency mode to prioritize high classification requests).
- Credential Update: The process by which administrators update their authentication or authorization data after Emergency mode.

ASSUMPTIONS REQUIRING CLARIFICATION
====================================
1. No further clarification needed regarding Emergency mode duration, triggering, privileges, mutually exclusive roles, or post-emergency delegation, as these have been defined per user clarifications. Any changes to the abstract nature of emergency privileges or mutually exclusive roles should be explicitly specified.

CONSTRAINTS
===========
- Only exactly 2 administrators may jointly trigger Emergency mode when the system is in Normal mode; each session may only trigger Emergency mode once.
- Emergency mode is time-limited by an abstract fixed duration and cannot persist indefinitely; the system must ensure eventual return to Normal mode. Early termination is permitted by the same 2 administrators who triggered Emergency mode.
- No new Emergency mode triggers are accepted while Emergency mode is active.
- During Emergency mode, request processing order may be bypassed to prioritize high classification requests; this relaxation is temporary.
- Users may temporarily hold predefined mutually exclusive roles only during Emergency mode.
- Only the administrators who triggered Emergency mode hold emergency privileges, defined as an abstract set of permissions, and only for the duration of Emergency mode.
- All roles delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked when Emergency mode ends. Roles delegated prior to Emergency mode persist until expiry or administrator revocation.
- Administrators who held emergency privileges are required to update their credentials upon exit from Emergency mode before resuming normal operations.
```

### ABSTRACTION GUIDANCE:
Ask questions only when the answer affects what must be verified, not how the requirement is implemented or modeled.

Ask when:
- Behavioral ambiguity affects verification
- Cardinality affects consistency
- Temporal ordering matters
- Missing scenarios need confirmation
- A requirement may be ambiguous, inconsistent, incomplete, or logically weak.


Do not ask when:
- the issue is only an implementation or modeling choice,
- the detail can be safely abstracted,
- different answers would not change the verification structure.

### DEFAULT ASSUMPTION POLICY
If the user does not answer a clarification question, continue with the smallest reasonable assumption.

Use the weakest assumption that:
- is enough to proceed,
- preserves the abstract requirement intent,
- and adds no unnecessary constraints or behavior.

State the assumption explicitly and treat it as provisional.

