- Step 3: Remove any nested let-bindings for start or timeoutT now made redundant.
- Step 4: Ensure that all references to timeoutT within the block (including at line 232) are inside the scope of this outer let-block.

[RATIONALE]:
This fix is Alloy 6 compliant because it uses a multi-variable let binding at the top level of the quantification block, ensuring that timeoutT is in scope wherever referenced; this approach is distinct from prior failed attempts which only adjusted let syntax or quantification structure without correcting the scoping.

[LESSON]:
When using let-bindings in Alloy, especially for variables needed in multiple expressions, bind them as early and as high up in the block as possible to ensure proper scope throughout.
