The error arises from the expression s.session = session within the context of all s: State | (s.session = session and TO/gte[s.t, et.endedAt]) => s.mode = NormalMode. Although Alloy allows field access via dot notation, the issue is that s.session yields a singleton set (of type Session), while session is an atom (also of type Session). In Alloy, comparing a singleton set to an atom using = is legal, but in some contexts—especially inside a larger boolean expression—Alloy may misinterpret the lack of explicit set containment, attempting a relational join (s . session), which is invalid. Prior attempts to fix this by adding parentheses or changing operator precedence failed because the underlying type mismatch was not directly addressed.

[FIX INTENT]:
Replace the equality s.session = session with session in s.session, making the set/atom relationship explicit and avoiding ambiguity in Alloy’s parser.

[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:
- Step 1: In fact EmergencyModeDurationAndExit, locate the line:
      some et.endedAt => all s: State | (s.session = session and TO/gte[s.t, et.endedAt]) => s.mode = NormalMode
- Step 2: Change s.session = session to session in s.session throughout the block, so the line reads:
      some et.endedAt => all s: State | (session in s.session and TO/gte[s.t, et.endedAt]) => s.mode = NormalMode
- Step 3: Similarly, review other lines in the block where s.session = session appears and, if the same context applies, replace with session in s.session for consistency and to preempt similar errors.

[RATIONALE]:
This fix is Alloy 6 compliant, resolves the type mismatch by making set membership explicit, and differs from prior failed attempts that only added parentheses or altered logical grouping without addressing the set/atom distinction.

[LESSON]:
When comparing a field that yields a singleton set to an atom in Alloy, use the in operator (atom in singleton set) to avoid ambiguity and type errors in boolean expressions.
